--TEST--
`#[Db\Derive]` refuses a field whose declared type is not a column at the property that declared it, not at the `queryAs<T>` that would later run — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`'s type map is the row format's reachable set, and it is not
// `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`'s: a row is a flat list of columns, so a nested object is not a
// column type however well it maps to JSON. `Handle` carries a derive of its
// own and is still refused here, which is the half that a rule copied from the
// JSON pass would get wrong — § 2's "another class that itself has a codec"
// admits exactly this field for `#[Json\Derive]`.
//
// `$cache` is a `Handle` too, and § 3's escape hatch takes it off the mapping
// in writing, so it is examined and accepted before `$h` is reached: a rule
// refusing the type's *appearance* rather than its presence in the contract
// would name both properties and still look right on the second half alone.
#[Core\Db\Derive]
class Row {
    #[Core\Db\Field(skip: true)]
    public Handle $cache;

    public Handle $h;

    public function constructor(Handle $cache, Handle $h)
    {
        $this->cache = $cache;
        $this->h = $h;
    }
}

#[Core\Db\Derive]
class Handle {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n)
    {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

echo "unreachable ", (new Row(new Handle(1), new Handle(2)))->h->n, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0756]: `Row::$h` is declared `Handle`, which has no column mapping
   --> case.nvs:18:19
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
